 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.658,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "How fast is this spark? A top expert doubted anyone could time it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.5,
   "dur": 8.822,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "It's a nerve signal, racing down one cell's thin fibre. Some fibres reach from spine to toe: about a metre. How long does that take?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 15.1,
   "dur": 9.612,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "Johannes Müller thought it might be as fast as light. In eighteen fifty, his former student Helmholtz timed it. About thirty metres a second: cheetah speed."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 25.5,
   "dur": 13.986,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "It isn't current in a wire: the cell re-fires it, step by step. Many fibres wear a fatty coat, myelin, and only re-fire at its gaps, so the signal leaps. Where it's damaged, as in multiple sclerosis, signals slow or stall."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 41.9,
   "dur": 8.678,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "The thinnest bare fibres crawl at about a metre a second. Thick wrapped ones, up to a hundred and fifty. Faster than a Formula One car."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 53.9,
   "dur": 5.848,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So a stubbed toe can hurt twice. The jolt rides faster fibres; the ache, bare ones."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 60.7,
   "dur": 5.929,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, a heart at rest beat about eighty times. Each began with a spark."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 67.5,
   "dur": 4.299,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: the end of the line. A gap most signals can't jump."
  }
 ]
}